1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,400
2,027 hours of work a year.

2
00:00:02,400 --> 00:00:04,200
By the time we leave, it's long been dark.

3
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:05,767
This is the Ministry of Labor's figure:

4
00:00:05,767 --> 00:00:07,900
total annual working hours for 2025.

5
00:00:07,900 --> 00:00:09,100
At eight hours a day,

6
00:00:09,100 --> 00:00:11,300
that is 253 working days.

7
00:00:11,300 --> 00:00:12,633
In 2014 it was longer:

8
00:00:12,633 --> 00:00:14,533
2,135 hours.

9
00:00:14,533 --> 00:00:15,433
Then it kept falling.

10
00:00:15,433 --> 00:00:17,067
In the pandemic year it dipped below 2,000.

11
00:00:17,067 --> 00:00:18,300
Then it climbed again.

12
00:00:18,300 --> 00:00:19,300
Last year brought more holidays,

13
00:00:19,300 --> 00:00:21,633
yet hours fell by just 3.6.

14
00:00:21,633 --> 00:00:22,967
Why won't it come down?

15
00:00:22,967 --> 00:00:24,500
Nearly all of us work full time.

16
00:00:24,500 --> 00:00:26,467
Part-timers are only 3 to 4 percent here.

17
00:00:26,467 --> 00:00:28,267
In Japan, about one in four.

18
00:00:28,267 --> 00:00:29,467
Then there is overtime.

19
00:00:29,467 --> 00:00:31,033
Regular hours fell last year,

20
00:00:31,033 --> 00:00:32,367
but overtime rose.

21
00:00:32,367 --> 00:00:33,667
And then there is after work.

22
00:00:33,667 --> 00:00:34,700
One in four

23
00:00:34,700 --> 00:00:36,800
got a message assigning them work.

24
00:00:36,800 --> 00:00:38,500
But this number needs careful reading.

25
00:00:38,500 --> 00:00:39,433
Full-time and part-time workers

26
00:00:39,433 --> 00:00:40,600
are averaged together.

27
00:00:40,600 --> 00:00:41,533
So we can't simply say

28
00:00:41,533 --> 00:00:42,367
that we work nearly

29
00:00:42,367 --> 00:00:44,567
400 hours more than people in Japan.

30
00:00:44,567 --> 00:00:46,600
Looking at full-timers only,

31
00:00:46,600 --> 00:00:47,600
Taiwan's week is 41.5 hours,

32
00:00:47,600 --> 00:00:48,867
shorter than Japan's.

33
00:00:48,867 --> 00:00:50,900
Next time: rent.
